WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.592 --> 00:00:07.592
For more than a century physicists have tried to find a unified theory a

00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:12.962
single mathematical framework capable of describing all of nature's forces

00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:18.692
Albert Einstein who unified space and time in a special and general theories of relativity

00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:26.102
Pursued this goal for over 30 years never succeeding today scientists are continuing the quest

00:00:26.623 --> 00:00:33.752
Seeking to combine general relativity describing the universe on large scale stars and galaxies with quantum mechanics

00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:36.243
describing the universe on subatomic scales

00:00:36.853 --> 00:00:40.952
Each of these two theories works amazingly well in its own domain

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:46.322
But put them together like in the dense center of a black hole and the math falls apart

00:00:46.993 --> 00:00:48.993
in the 1970s

00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:54.122
Scientists came upon a potential solution, but from an unexpected starting point

00:00:54.673 --> 00:01:00.423
Studies of nuclear processes in that era we were trying to understand the strong nuclear force

00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:03.213
It's just the force that holds the nucleus of the atom

00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:08.883
Together and that was a complete mystery how to do it in trying to solve that mystery

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:13.592
Physicists were led to a promising equation that seemed to describe

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:18.122
Vibrating strings that would hold together atomic constituents

00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:23.343
but within just a few years a different theory proved to be the right solution for

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:28.262
Understanding the strong nuclear force and so strings no longer seemed necessary

00:01:28.873 --> 00:01:31.983
Almost all of these several hundred people stopped working on

00:01:32.623 --> 00:01:40.233
String theory at that time and only a handful of diehards continued to pursue them the few of us who continued working on string theory

00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:45.632
Felt that the mathematics was so beautiful and compelling that this theory had to be good for something

00:01:46.182 --> 00:01:50.641
Much like Einstein who spent long periods working on ideas outside the mainstream

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:53.522
Schwartz and his colleague Joelle shirk

00:01:54.042 --> 00:01:56.192
Continued to explore string theory

00:01:56.462 --> 00:02:02.311
We realized what string theory was good for and that was as a quantum theory of gravity at that point

00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:05.132
I was convinced that this is what I would be worried to the rest of my career

00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:11.162
Schwartz was proposing that string theory which had failed as a theory of the strong nuclear force was

00:02:11.323 --> 00:02:15.183
Actually the solution to one of the biggest problems in all of physics

00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:17.733
uniting gravity and quantum mechanics

00:02:18.313 --> 00:02:23.642
When Schwartz excitedly announced this prospect few people paid any attention

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:27.123
The field just wasn't ready. So

00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:35.912
Schwartz and a new convert Michael Green spent years developing the theory mostly on their own then in

00:02:36.493 --> 00:02:43.412
1984 they achieved a breakthrough showing that the mathematics of string theory deftly avoided potentially lethal

00:02:43.693 --> 00:02:48.633
Technical issues and this time the community of physicists heard them

00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:52.682
Princeton between the University and the Institute for Advanced Study

00:02:53.202 --> 00:02:56.672
You had almost half the experts in the world. Who were

00:02:57.313 --> 00:03:00.122
working on related topics and I

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:08.373
Am told that at that point almost all of them dropped what they were doing and became string theorists as string theory rapidly progressed

00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:15.662
There was a sense that research was but a few years away from uncovering the final and unified theory of physics

00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:23.373
more than 30 years later the goal of a fully unified and thoroughly tested theory has yet to be achieved but

00:03:23.863 --> 00:03:25.863
enthusiasm remains high

00:03:26.232 --> 00:03:33.092
String theory has come closer than any other attempt to go beyond known physics and tackled the grand challenge of unification

00:03:34.303 --> 00:03:38.133
But the question remains is string theory and elegant mathematical

00:03:38.743 --> 00:03:44.732
Chimera, or our string theorist rapidly approaching the realization of einstein's dream

